  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nOur method uses a hybrid optimization strategy combining random projection and stochastic gradient descent (SGD) with careful hyperparameter tuning. We start by interpolating the parent solution to multiple grid sizes and then locally optimize each. The core idea is to seek a function structure that minimizes overlap by avoiding correlated patterns. We improve upon the Adam optimizer by incorporating a momentum mix and adaptive learning rate annealing. The key bottleneck addressed is the tendency to converge to local optima, which we mitigate via stochasticity and multi-resolution starting points.\n\nMathematical specification\nOur representation: step-function on grid [0,2] with N samples, vector h \u2208 [0,1]^N with \u2211h = N/2.\n\nObjective: \nminimize C5(N) = max_{k} (dx * |\u27e8h(x), 1-h(x+k)\u27e9|)\n\nwhere dx=2.0/N, and use scipy.correlate with mode='full' for correlation.\n\nConstraints:\n0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n\u2211_i h_i = N/2\n\nOptimization details (SGD variant):\n- Variable \u03c3 = \u03b7 * \u03c4_{adapt}^{(t)} (effective learning rate)\n- Use gradient from hessian_lagrange to maintain physical interpretation\n- Project gradient using beam search strategy\n- Learning rate annealing: 0.999^t * base_lr for t steps\n- Step selection: lag space weighted by gradient magnitude\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Input: initial_h_values, budget_s, seed\n2. Create parent interpolation {f:N \u2192 \u211d | f interpolates parent}\n3. For N in [95,190,380,760]:\n   a. Initialize candidate = interpolate_parent(N)\n   b. Adjust candidate parameters from seed/2\n   c. Run SGD optimize_h_ext(initial_candidate, N)\n   d. Track local mode improvements\n   e. Update global best solution C5 value\n4. Project successful candidates with integral constraint\n5. Final polishing with Adam optimizer\n\nSearch choices and tuning\nGrid sizes: [95,190,380,760]\nSuggested SG:\n- base_lr: 0.03\n- decay rate: 0.85 per menstrual\n- \u03c4_adapt_max: 10.0 * N^(1/3)\n- Gradient threshold: 1e-5\n- Project step: ceil(0.01*N)\n\nComputebudgetandrobustness\nDominant cost: N^2 operations per evaluation (correlation). \nEstimate: Grid size N=760 uses ~200s (CPU-bound). Allocate 10/11 budget to largest grids.\nMonitoring: anometer calculation during hess_lag computation\nSafety: revert to parent interpolation on stagnation or exception\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Check h: 0\u2264h_i\u22641 and near-integral=N/2\n2. Compute C5 with scipy.correlate\n3. Return (h_best, C5, N)\n4. Include sample\u30a8\u30eb\u06af\u0627\u0646 checks:\n    - Project_sum_enforcer()\n    - C5_lower_bound_estimator()\n\nImplementation details\nHelper functions:\n- project_sum(target = N/2)\n- compute_c5(h, dx)\n- check_grad_lag(h, lag, N)  # finite-difference verifier\n\nGrid progression based on N:\nGrid sizes = [N0*d for d in [0.5,1,2,4]];\nwhere N0=190\n\nConstraint handling:\nIntegral enforced via projection + Lagrangian penalty\nBoundary: clip to [0,1] + integral correction if deviation > 1e-8\n\nAlgorithmic operations postponed:\n- gradient threshold setting\n- decomposition based on grid size\n- stochastic beam update\n\nBest practices followed:\n- Avoid gradient-based repairs\n- Use cumulative lag statistic for gradient weighing\n- Introduce noise as 10% with \u03c3 adaptive\n- Implement cooling schedule: \u03c4 = \u03c4_max * (0.9999)^t",
